  "research": {
    "area-3": {
      "text": "This area integrates office, retail, dining, and entertainment, suggesting a built-in audience and a walkable urban setting.\n\nThe Shops at Don Mills features a variety of retail stores and restaurants, including Eataly, McEwan Fine Foods, and several casual dining options.\n\nIndoor playgrounds are often classified as \"recreation use\" and may require specific zoning, a change-of-use permit, and adherence to building codes for assembly spaces.\n\nThe City of Toronto requires an Amusement Establishment license for businesses offering amusement activities, which involves a zoning review, liability insurance, and potentially TSSA licenses for certain equipment.\n\nPlanning for sufficient space for play, seating, and required facilities such as washrooms and exits is critical, as these factors determine occupant load.\n\nAdditionally, parents are generally required to remain on-site unless the business is a licensed child care centre.\n\nThe cost of building and fitting out such a space in Toronto can be significant.\n\nFor example, Kitaya Japanese Takeout, Asian Legend, CoCo, Diwan, and Sorento Italian Kitchen are all located nearby.\n\nSpecific restaurants mentioned within the Shops at Don Mills include Anejo Restaurant, Chipotle, Danish Pastry House, McEwan Fine Foods, and Eataly.\n\nCasual dining options like Jack Astor's Bar & Grill are also present.\n\nSeveral existing play cafés in Toronto, such as Pinocchio Play Café in Lawrence Park, The Willow Play Cafe, and Oaks 'n Acorns, demonstrate the concept's presence in the city.\n\nAccess and local context\n\nThe Shops at Don Mills is located at Don Mills Road and Lawrence Avenue East, offering good connectivity with proximity to the Don Valley Parkway and public transit routes.\n\nThe centre provides on-site parking, including surface lots and a multi-level parkade.\n\nThe surrounding area includes various commercial establishments along Don Mills Road, such as the Peanut Plaza further north, which features a supermarket and other retailers.\n\nThe \"lifestyle centre\" format of Shops at Don Mills, with its internal streets and town square, aims to create a neighborhood retail feel.\n\nKey questions include:\n\n*   What are the zoning classifications for potential sites and do they permit \"recreation use\" or \"amusement centre\" with or without conditional use permits?\n\n*   What are the requirements for building permits for a change of use from retail to an assembly occupancy, and what fire safety and building code standards apply to an indoor playground?\n\n*   What is the clear ceiling height of potential units, and is it sufficient for indoor playground equipment?\n\n*   What is the maximum occupant load for different areas of the proposed space, and are there adequate exits and washroom facilities for that capacity?\n\n*   What are the specific requirements for an Amusement Establishment license from the City of Toronto, including liability insurance and any necessary TSSA permits?\n\n*   Are there any local regulations regarding the operation of a café alongside an indoor playground, especially concerning food preparation and health permits?",
